> ### 3. The completed `extract_values_for_obs_row`
> 
> Here is the function in full. It:
> 
> - Takes a `pandas.Series` row with `lat`, `lon`, `year`, `month`.
> - Looks up model outputs from each dataset.
> - Returns a dict with all the desired variables, defaulting to `NaN` if anything fails.
> 
> ```python
> def extract_values_for_obs_row(
>     row,
>     ds_ttp=None,
>     ds_runoff=None,
>     ds_flow=None,
> ):
>     """
>     Given a single observation row with columns:
>         lat, lon, year, month
>     extract model values from the provided xarray datasets.
> 
>     Returns a dict with keys:
>         tp, tmean, rainfall, era5melt, tempmelt,
>         rainfall_flow, era5melt_flow, tempmelt_flow
>     """
>     lat = float(row["lat"])
>     lon = float(row["lon"])
>     year = int(row["year"])
>     month = int(row["month"])
> 
>     result = {
>         "tp": np.nan,
>         "tmean": np.nan,
>         "rainfall": np.nan,
>         "era5melt": np.nan,
>         "tempmelt": np.nan,
>         "rainfall_flow": np.nan,
>         "era5melt_flow": np.nan,
>         "tempmelt_flow": np.nan,
>     }
> 
>     # --- 3.1 ttp: tp, tmean ---
>     if ds_ttp is not None:
>         try:
>             # nearest grid cell in space
>             da = ds_ttp.sel(latitude=lat, longitude=lon, method="nearest")
