**assistant**:
> You’re hitting that merge/concat error because we’re trying to concatenate regional datasets that each have a *coordinate variable* called `year` on the `time` dimension, and xarray wants those coordinates to agree wherever dimensions align. Across regions they don’t, so `xr.concat` complains.
> 
> Key point: you don’t actually need a `year` coordinate on the datasets at all; you can just use the native `time` coordinate (already monthly) and match by year/month in the pandas side, or derive `year`/`month` *after* concatenation.
> 
> There are two clean options:
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Option A (simplest): drop `year`/`month` from the xarray side
> 
> Change the loader so we never add `year` and `month` coords to the regional datasets.
> 
> Concretely, **remove or comment out** this in the previous code:
> 
> ```python
> ds_ttp = ds_ttp.assign_coords(
>     year=("time", ds["time"].dt.year.values),
>     month=("time", ds["time"].dt.month.values),
> )
> ```
> 
> and similarly for `ds_runoff` and `ds_flow`.
> 
> Then adjust `extract_values_for_obs_row` to match on `time` directly instead of `year`/`month`:
> 
> ```python
> def extract_values_for_obs_row(
>     row,
>     ds_ttp=None,
>     ds_runoff=None,
>     ds_flow=None,
> ):
>     lat = float(row["lat"])
>     lon = float(row["lon"])
>     year = int(row["year"])
>     month = int(row["month"])
> 
>     result = {
>         "tp": np.nan,
>         "tmean": np.nan,
>         "rainfall": np.nan,
>         "era5melt": np.nan,
>         "tempmelt": np.nan,
>         "rainfall_flow": np.nan,
>         "era5melt_flow": np.nan,
>         "tempmelt_flow": np.nan,
>     }
> 
>     # build the target month start as a pandas Timestamp
>     import pandas as pd
>     target_time = pd.Timestamp(year=year, month=month, day=1)
> 
>     # helper closure
>     def _time_filter(da):
>         # match exact month, or nearest within a tolerance if you prefer
>         # exact monthly grid:
>         return da.sel(time=target_time, method="nearest")
> 
>     # --- tp, tmean ---
>     if ds_ttp is not None:
>         try:
>             da = ds_ttp.sel(latitude=lat, longitude=lon, method="nearest")
>             da_t = _time_filter(da)
>             result["tp"]    = float(da_t["tp"].values.mean())
>             result["tmean"] = float(da_t["tmean"].values.mean())
>         except Exception:
>             pass
> 
>     # --- runoff rainfall, era5melt, tempmelt ---
>     if ds_runoff is not None:
>         try:
>             da = ds_runoff.sel(latitude=lat, longitude=lon, method="nearest")
>             da_t = _time_filter(da)
>             result["rainfall"] = float(da_t["rainfall"].values.mean())
>             result["era5melt"] = float(da_t["era5melt"].values.mean())
>             result["tempmelt"] = float(da_t["tempmelt"].values.mean())
>         except Exception:
>             pass
> 
>     # --- flow rainfall_flow, era5melt_flow, tempmelt_flow ---
>     if ds_flow is not None:
>         try:
>             da = ds_flow.sel(latitude=lat, longitude=lon, method="nearest")
>             da_t = _time_filter(da)
>             result["rainfall_flow"] = float(da_t["rainfall_flow"].values.mean())
>             result["era5melt_flow"] = float(da_t["era5melt_flow"].values.mean())
>             result["tempmelt_flow"] = float(da_t["tempmelt_flow"].values.mean())
>         except Exception:
>             pass
> 
>     return result
> ```
> 
> Notes:
> 
> - `sel(time=target_time, method="nearest")` will naturally handle:
>   - Regions that don’t cover that year at all → raises or returns nearest outside range; if you want “NaN if outside range”, we can check `time.min()`/`max()` first.
> - No `year` coordinate is present, so no conflict on concat.
> 
> If you want to be stricter (only accept exact month, not nearest), replace the `_time_filter` with something like:
> 
> ```python
> def _time_filter(da):
>     # exact equality on year/month
>     t = da["time"]
>     mask = (t.dt.year == year) & (t.dt.month == month)
>     da_tm = da.where(mask, drop=True)
>     if da_tm.sizes.get("time", 0) == 0:
>         raise KeyError("No matching time")
>     return da_tm.isel(time=0)  # if there are multiple, just take first
> ```
> 
> That fully avoids having a `year` variable on the xarray side.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Option B: keep `year`/`month`, but drop them before concatenation
> 
> If you like having `year` and `month` in each regional dataset for debugging, you can keep them during loading but **drop them just before concatenation** so that `ds_ttp_global` et al. don’t carry those coordinates:
> 
> ```python
> # after building ttp_datasets list:
> ttp_datasets_no_ym = [ds.drop_vars(["year", "month"]) for ds in ttp_datasets]
> runoff_datasets_no_ym = [ds.drop_vars(["year", "month"]) for ds in runoff_datasets]
> flow_datasets_no_ym = [ds.drop_vars(["year", "month"]) for ds in flow_datasets]
> 
> ds_ttp_global    = xr.concat(ttp_datasets_no_ym,    dim="region") if ttp_datasets    else None
> ds_runoff_global = xr.concat(runoff_datasets_no_ym, dim="region") if runoff_datasets else None
> ds_flow_global   = xr.concat(flow_datasets_no_ym,   dim="region") if flow_datasets   else None
> ```
> 
> Then use the `time`-based matching in `extract_values_for_obs_row` exactly as in Option A.
> 
> ---
> 
> Given your note that:
> 
> > The years are not consistent across the regions, and they may not overlap with all of the years that the streamflow is available.
> 
> I recommend:
> 
> - Option A or B (time-based matching, no `year` coord in concatenated datasets).
> - Use the strict `_time_filter` variant that returns NaN when there is no exact month match, so you clearly see gaps rather than “nearest” years being pulled in.
> 
> If you paste one example `print(ds_ttp)` and `print(ds_flow)` I can give you a minimal, precise `_time_filter` that’s guaranteed to align with how those time axes are defined.
